# wfg-log 1
# fixture rounds — a shuffled ambience bed, pruned during the show and left at a
# boundary, hand-checked against a live session record for record.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE IS FOR. §3.6 lets a group shuffle its members, play a subset
# of them, loop the result for ever, and have a member dropped from the run
# without the show being edited. Every one of those is a DECISION the engine
# takes rather than a fact the document states — and one of them is taken with a
# random number generator.
#
# So each round is written down. `run.round` carries the seed the run is drawing
# from and the members in the order they will play, and a replay reads that back
# rather than drawing it: the generator is consulted on the night and never
# again. That is the same guarantee a generated identifier has, applied to an
# order.
#
# THE SEED IS THE DOCUMENT'S, seven, which is how a shuffled scene gets
# rehearsed: the same order at every performance. Left at nought instead, a
# fresh one is drawn when the run starts and written into the log — so the show
# is different every night AND every night reproduces exactly.
#
# The group loops for ever, which an ambience bed does, and the members are
# memos so a round takes nine ticks rather than a file's length.
#
# GO fires the group the pointer is on.
A 0 0 cli go s:"RN000001"
#
# ROUND ONE, drawn before anything is spawned. The three members in an order
# nobody wrote — with this seed, the order they happen to be written in.
A 1 1 engine run.round s:"RN000001" i:7 s:"BED00003" s:"BED00004" s:"BED00005"
A 1 2 engine run.spawn s:"RN000001" s:"BED00003" s:"RN000002"
A 2 3 engine run.launch s:"RN000002"
A 3 4 engine run.ended s:"RN000002"
A 4 5 engine run.spawn s:"RN000001" s:"BED00004" s:"RN000003"
A 5 6 engine run.launch s:"RN000003"
A 6 7 engine run.ended s:"RN000003"
A 7 8 engine run.spawn s:"RN000001" s:"BED00005" s:"RN000004"
A 8 9 engine run.launch s:"RN000004"
A 9 10 engine run.ended s:"RN000004"
#
# ROUND TWO, drawn on the tick the last member of round one finished — and
# obeying §3.6's boundary constraint, which is the half of shuffling that is not
# obvious: the first of a new round may not be the last of the one before it.
# Round one ended on `BED00005`, so this one may not begin there, and it does
# not. A fresh draw is otherwise perfectly entitled to start with the member
# that has just finished, and hearing the same ambience twice running is exactly
# what shuffling was asked for to avoid.
A 10 11 engine run.round s:"RN000001" i:7 s:"BED00003" s:"BED00004" s:"BED00005"
A 10 12 engine run.spawn s:"RN000001" s:"BED00003" s:"RN000005"
#
# AND HERE IS 22:40. One of the three is wrong tonight, and the operator drops
# it — from this RUN, not from the show. `run.prune` is not an edit: the cue
# stays in the group, enabled, and tomorrow it is back (§4.10 — the document
# holds what somebody decided, and this is not that).
#
# It reaches the round already in progress, which is the whole point of asking:
# `BED00004` was second in the order drawn a moment ago and it does not play.
# The round jumps from `BED00003` straight to `BED00005`.
A 11 13 cli run.prune s:"RN000001" s:"BED00004" s:"group"
A 11 14 engine run.launch s:"RN000005"
A 12 15 engine run.ended s:"RN000005"
A 13 16 engine run.spawn s:"RN000001" s:"BED00005" s:"RN000006"
A 14 17 engine run.launch s:"RN000006"
A 15 18 engine run.ended s:"RN000006"
#
# ROUND THREE is drawn from what is left, which is two members — and with two,
# the boundary constraint decides the order completely. That is correct rather
# than degenerate: alternating is what somebody asking for two shuffled
# ambiences means.
A 16 19 engine run.round s:"RN000001" i:7 s:"BED00003" s:"BED00005"
A 16 20 engine run.spawn s:"RN000001" s:"BED00003" s:"RN000007"
A 17 21 engine run.launch s:"RN000007"
A 18 22 engine run.ended s:"RN000007"
A 19 23 engine run.spawn s:"RN000001" s:"BED00005" s:"RN000008"
A 20 24 engine run.launch s:"RN000008"
A 21 25 engine run.ended s:"RN000008"
#
# AND THE WAY OUT OF FOR EVER. `afterIteration` lets the scene reach a boundary
# it was going to reach anyway and stop there — which is how an infinite loop is
# left without a cut. `run.kill` would have taken it now, mid-cue, and that is
# the emergency path rather than this one.
#
# IT COSTS ONE MORE ROUND HERE, and the reason is worth reading rather than
# discovering. The scheduler decides in a tick hook, which runs BEFORE the
# tick's commands are applied — so on tick 22 it had already decided to start
# another round when this arrived in the same tick's queue. A press one tick
# earlier, twenty milliseconds, would have been seen. It is the same one-tick
# decision latency `/godot/engine/sequenceGapTicks` publishes, seen from the
# other side, and it is the price of every decision being a logged command.
A 22 26 cli run.stop s:"RN000001" s:"afterIteration"
A 22 27 engine run.round s:"RN000001" i:7 s:"BED00003" s:"BED00005"
A 22 28 engine run.spawn s:"RN000001" s:"BED00003" s:"RN000009"
A 23 29 engine run.launch s:"RN000009"
A 24 30 engine run.ended s:"RN000009"
A 25 31 engine run.spawn s:"RN000001" s:"BED00005" s:"RN000010"
A 26 32 engine run.launch s:"RN000010"
A 27 33 engine run.ended s:"RN000010"
#
# That round finished, and no other began. The group has no footer, so it
# reports done on the next tick.
A 28 34 engine run.ended s:"RN000001"
